metalive wrote:
> Hello!

Hi!

> I am very new to the game and I was wondering if you could answer the
> following questions for me.
> 
> 1. How do I know how much damage a weapon does. For example in Diablo
> each weapon's damage number is indicated. In NH, I often don't know
> which weapon to wield, because I don't know which one's better. Should
> I wield my axe, or this sword, or perhaps this club ...which one will
> do more damage?

You have to figure that out for yourself. There are guides available on 
the net (http://www.steelypips.org/nethack is one site that comes to 
mind), but part of the fun is discovering things for yourself.

If you've played D&D or Neverwinter Nights, many of the weapons (IIRC) 
share the same stats.

> 2. What are statues, sinks, altars and wells/fountains (good) for? OK,
> I know a few things about them, for example, that one can #dip random
> things into a fountain and see what happens, and that one can find
> items under statues. But how? I tried (s)earch while standing on a
> statue, but nothing ever happens?

Statues:
Try breaking them up. Use the kind of tool you would use to break rocks.

Sinks:
Dangerous, but occasionally useful. Unclogging the pipes might leave you 
with a mess on your hands.

Fountains:
You can drink from them ('q'uaff), but you're very likely to have 
something bad (often fatal) happen to you if you do so, unless your 
level is quite high. If you are a Lawful character, fountains have an 
additional effect; let's just say that it seems like the Lady of the 
Lake has moved house.

Altars:
Read the text you see at the beginning of the game, carefully.
Read the help as well, and check out some of the #-commands. Altars are 
one of the most useful and powerful dungeon features in the game, once 
you know how to use them.

> What about those other things (altars, thrones, sinks,
> engravement/grafitti), what are they good for?

Altars/sinks: already mentioned.

Thrones: well, what would you normally do with a throne? Check out the 
#-commands.

Grafitti: Sometimes it has useful hints; sometimes the hints are fake. 
When you find grafitti in a corridor, do not do any kicking nearby 
(hint! hint!).

Engravement: What should you engrave? They say there is a Name which 
causes enemies to flee in terror. Read a very, very famous fantasy 
trilogy to find it.


You can also get many useful gameplay hints by seeking out the Oracle 
and paying for a consultation. She is located somewhere in the main 
dungeons, on a level with a special layout. Eating fortune cookies 
occasionally gives you a useful tip too.

> 3. How do the different races affect the game(play)? I have never
> noticed any difference, allthough I have played them all at least a
> few times. I only noticed, that orcs need much more food!?

IIRC your starting stats are modified by the race you pick.
Elves have infravision (= can see most enemies, even in the dark).
Dwarves can dig tunnels faster than other races (they still need tools, 
though).
Also, if you're playing a particular race, members of that race will 
usually be friendly - except for humans! Hence there's a part of the 
dungeon where gnomes have a great advantage...

Good beginner classes are the Barbarian and Valkyrie. I haven't played 
much with Barbs, but the Dwarven or Human Valks are both good classes to 
play.

> 4. I think I am stuck on Dlvl 6. There are two small rooms connected
> by a corridor. I have searched at least 20 turns on each and every
> spot of the wall, but there seems to be no secret door.

There's always a door. Is the corridor diagonal? - Try walking around 
there and see if you missed discovering a side passage. If there's a 
T-junction leading to a dead end, there might be a hidden passage on the 
other side of the dead end; search there.

In the worst case, try and find yourself a digging implement and make 
your OWN passage down to Dlvl 7. You should be able to find one by going 
back up to about Dlvl 2-4 (I think) and searching for a second 
down-staircase.

